### Generated by Generative AI

OpenAI Codex CLI 0.162.1 (model `gpt-6-sol`) assisted with the reproducer, source analysis, and issue text.

### Operating System:

Ubuntu 24.04.5 LTS / x86_64 (Jazzy isolated rootfs); Ubuntu 26.04.1 LTS / x86_64 (Rolling isolated rootfs).

### ROS version or commit hash:

Source-built rclcpp Jazzy 28.1.22 at `53fa7aeea86e8ed484f9465fa14059faf13a71f1`; Rolling 33.1.0 at `e7363f1027d5ea391500359bc1a2b17cb9d8d66f`.

### RMW implementation (if applicable):

`rmw_fastrtps_cpp` (Fast DDS) in both runs.

### RMW Configuration (if applicable):

Default RMW configuration; `ROS_LOCALHOST_ONLY=1`, isolated `ROS_DOMAIN_ID` per run.

### Client library (if applicable):

`rclcpp`.

### 'ros2 doctor --report' output

Not run inside the isolated rootfs. The source-built rclcpp commits, distro-specific underlays, operating systems, and RMW used for the reproducer are given above.

### Steps to reproduce issue

1. Build the indicated rclcpp commit against its matching Jazzy or Rolling underlay. For a conventional installed workspace, source `/opt/ros/<distro>/setup.bash` and then the source-built workspace's `install/setup.bash`, and verify that both the headers and `librclcpp` come from the selected source build. The actual isolated runs below instead sourced only the matching underlay and explicitly selected the source-built header and library paths; they did not source an install overlay.
2. Put this complete file in `repro.cpp`:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <memory>
#include <stdexcept>
#include <string>
#include "rclcpp/rclcpp.hpp"
#include "std_msgs/msg/string.hpp"

struct ThrowingMessage
{
  std::string text;
  inline static bool fail_copy = false;
  ThrowingMessage() = default;
  ThrowingMessage(const ThrowingMessage & other) : text(other.text)
  {
    if (fail_copy) {throw std::runtime_error("controlled custom copy failure");}
  }
};

template<>
struct rclcpp::TypeAdapter<ThrowingMessage, std_msgs::msg::String>
{
  using is_specialized = std::true_type;
  using custom_type = ThrowingMessage;
  using ros_message_type = std_msgs::msg::String;
  static void convert_to_ros_message(const custom_type & source, ros_message_type & destination)
  {destination.data = source.text;}
  static void convert_to_custom(const ros_message_type & source, custom_type & destination)
  {destination.text = source.data;}
};

int main(int argc, char ** argv)
{
  rclcpp::init(argc, argv);
  int exit_code = 0;
  {
    using Adapter = rclcpp::TypeAdapter<ThrowingMessage, std_msgs::msg::String>;
    auto node = std::make_shared<rclcpp::Node>(
      "copy_exception", rclcpp::NodeOptions().use_intra_process_comms(true));
    auto pub = node->create_publisher<Adapter>("copy", 10);
    auto a = node->create_subscription<Adapter>("copy", 10,
      [](std::unique_ptr<ThrowingMessage>) {});
    auto b = node->create_subscription<Adapter>("copy", 10,
      [](std::unique_ptr<ThrowingMessage>) {});
    ThrowingMessage::fail_copy = true;
    try {
      pub->publish(std::make_unique<ThrowingMessage>());
      std::cerr << "copy exception was not propagated\n";
      exit_code = 2;
    } catch (const std::runtime_error & error) {
      std::cout << "expected exception: " << error.what() << std::endl;
    }
  }
  rclcpp::shutdown();
  return exit_code;
}
```

3. In the same directory, create `CMakeLists.txt`:

```cmake
cmake_minimum_required(VERSION 3.16)
project(ipc_copy_exception_leak_repro)
find_package(ament_cmake REQUIRED)
find_package(rclcpp REQUIRED)
find_package(std_msgs REQUIRED)
add_executable(repro repro.cpp)
ament_target_dependencies(repro rclcpp std_msgs)
```

4. Configure, build, and run with LeakSanitizer enabled. Ensure the selected headers and `librclcpp` both come from the indicated source build:

```bash
cmake -S . -B build \
  -DCMAKE_CXX_FLAGS='-fsanitize=address,undefined -fno-omit-frame-pointer -g' \
  -DCMAKE_EXE_LINKER_FLAGS='-fsanitize=address,undefined'
cmake --build build -j2
ASAN_OPTIONS=detect_leaks=1:halt_on_error=1 ./build/repro
```

The recorded isolated runs used this environment and source selection (shown with the paths mounted inside each rootfs):

```bash
export ROS_DISTRO=rolling  # or jazzy, with its matching rootfs and source tree
source /opt/ros/$ROS_DISTRO/setup.bash
export LD_LIBRARY_PATH=/tmp/previous/runs/$ROS_DISTRO-baseline/build/rclcpp:${LD_LIBRARY_PATH:-}
cmake -S /tmp/task -B /tmp/ws/build \
  -DRCLCPP_SOURCE=/tmp/source \
  -DSOURCE_BUILD=/tmp/previous/runs/$ROS_DISTRO-baseline/build \
  -DSANITIZER=address,undefined
cmake --build /tmp/ws/build -j2 --target exception_probe
ASAN_OPTIONS=detect_leaks=1:halt_on_error=1 \
  UBSAN_OPTIONS=halt_on_error=1 /tmp/ws/build/exception_probe
```

Here `/tmp/task` contains the standalone `exception_probe.cpp` and a harness `CMakeLists.txt` that puts `/tmp/source/rclcpp/include` before underlay headers and points imported `rclcpp::rclcpp` to `${SOURCE_BUILD}/rclcpp/librclcpp.so`. The simpler CMake file above assumes a correctly sourced conventional overlay. The source selection above is what produced the logs below; an `install/setup.bash` overlay was not sourced in the isolated runs.

The reproducer stores an unexpected no-exception result in `exit_code` and always calls `rclcpp::shutdown()` on that path. It only leaks when a copy constructor throws while a custom message is distributed to multiple owning intra-process buffers; a normal `publish()` is not claimed to leak.

### Expected behavior

`publish()` propagates the copy exception, and storage allocated for the failed copy is released. With the controlled exception, LeakSanitizer reports no leak.

### Actual behavior

Both unchanged source-built distributions propagate the exception and exit 1 under LeakSanitizer. Full allocation stacks from the final standalone reproducer runs:

